  "notes": [
    "The book divides the list into Early (1806-1808), Middle (1809-1812) and Late (1813-1814) periods. The builder doesn't enforce them: choices that belong to a period say so in their name, and the limits are the Middle and Late ones.",
    "Early-period limits that differ: up to two battalions in the army can be Light infantry and one Highlanders (rather than one in four), and up to two battalions per brigade Large (rather than one).",
    "The Light Brigade takes one further unit in the Middle period, up to two in the Late.",
    "Detached Elite Companies: a brigade's Line battalions can lose their Light companies (and Mixed Formation) and/or Grenadier companies (and 1 Hand-to-Hand). Two battalions' companies make a Tiny unit, three a Small unit, four a standard one, for any infantry brigade; a battalion that loses both becomes Small. Not built here.",
    "Household Cavalry saw little Peninsular action, so the book leaves them out of the lists, but you can take one to three at the listed cost.",
    "Brunswick Light Infantry is one unit per army, whether in an Infantry Brigade or the KGL Light Brigade; the builder checks each brigade type separately.",
    "The chapter (p. 48) makes KGL Light Infantry Reliable, Elite 4+; the list's table (p. 99) is followed.",
    "Half batteries cost what the Options boxes take off (Royal 15, Horse 16, Portuguese 13), with the half battery stat line on p. 101 (2-1-1, Stamina 1).",
    "Named commanders have no official points cost in this supplement; they're priced here at the Divisional Commander cost for their Staff Rating, so adjust as you agree."
  ],
